(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Define the primary functions of the Transport Layer and contrast process-to-process delivery with host-to-host delivery.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Transport Layer \(Layer 4 in the OSI and TCP/IP reference architectures\) is responsible for) Tj
T*
(   providing logical, end-to-end communication services between specific application processes) Tj
T*
(   running on distinct network hosts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Key functions include:) Tj
T*
(   1. Process-to-Process Delivery: While the Network Layer delivers packets from source host to) Tj
T*
(   destination host using IP addresses, the Transport Layer routes data to the specific) Tj
T*
(   application running on that host using 16-bit Port Numbers.) Tj
T*
(   2. Multiplexing and Demultiplexing: Multiplexing combines data streams from multiple local) Tj
T*
(   application sockets into transport segments, whereas Demultiplexing examines segment headers at) Tj
T*
(   the destination to route payload bytes to the correct target socket.) Tj
T*
(   3. Connection Management: Establishing, maintaining, and terminating logical communication) Tj
T*
(   sessions \(e.g., TCP 3-way handshake and 4-way teardown\).) Tj
T*
(   4. Flow Control: Regulating sender transmission rate via sliding window protocols to prevent) Tj
T*
(   buffer overflow at the receiver.) Tj
T*
(   5. Congestion Control: Throttling traffic to protect intermediate network routers and links) Tj
T*
(   from collapse.) Tj
T*
(   6. Error Detection and Recovery: Checksums detect bit errors; acknowledgments and) Tj
T*
(   retransmission timers guarantee reliable delivery.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the structure and categorization of Port Numbers in computer networking, detailing Well-Known, Registered, and Ephemeral ports.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Port numbers are 16-bit unsigned integers ranging from 0 to 65,535 \(2^16 values\) utilized to) Tj
T*
(   uniquely identify application processes running on a networked operating system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Internet Assigned Numbers Authority \(IANA\) divides port numbers into three standardized) Tj
T*
(   categories:) Tj
T*
(   1. Well-Known Ports \(0 to 1,023\): Reserved for ubiquitous, privileged system-level server) Tj
T*
(   services. Examples include HTTP \(Port 80\), HTTPS \(Port 443\), DNS \(Port 53\), SSH \(Port 22\),) Tj
T*
(   Telnet \(Port 23\), SMTP \(Port 25\), and FTP \(Ports 20/21\). On Unix-like systems, binding to) Tj
T*
(   well-known ports requires administrative \(root\) privileges.) Tj
T*
(   2. Registered Ports \(1,024 to 49,151\): Assigned to specific user-level processes and) Tj
T*
(   proprietary applications upon request to IANA to avoid conflicts. Examples include Microsoft) Tj
T*
(   SQL Server \(1433\), MySQL \(3306\), PostgreSQL \(5432\), and HTTP Alternate / Apache Tomcat \(8080\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Dynamic, Private, or Ephemeral Ports \(49,152 to 65,535\): Allocated dynamically by the client) Tj
T*
(   host's operating system stack when an application initiates an outbound connection. These ports) Tj
T*
(   are temporary and released back to the OS pool once the session terminates.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Compare and contrast Transmission Control Protocol \(TCP\) and User Datagram Protocol \(UDP\) across all fundamental operating parameters.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   TCP and UDP represent two fundamentally divergent transport layer design paradigms:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

