BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
45 780 Td
(   1. Connection Model: TCP is connection-oriented, requiring a formal 3-way handshake before data) Tj
T*
(   transfer. UDP is connectionless, transmitting datagrams immediately without session setup.) Tj
T*
(   2. Reliability: TCP provides guaranteed delivery via sequence numbering, positive cumulative) Tj
T*
(   ACKs, and retransmission timeouts \(RTO\). UDP is best-effort and unreliable; dropped packets are) Tj
T*
(   not recovered.) Tj
T*
(   3. Data Stream Model: TCP provides a continuous, unstructured byte-stream interface. UDP) Tj
T*
(   preserves message boundaries, delivering discrete application datagrams.) Tj
T*
(   4. Header Overhead: TCP headers are 20 to 60 bytes with complex control fields. UDP headers are) Tj
T*
(   minimal and fixed at exactly 8 bytes.) Tj
T*
(   5. Flow and Congestion Control: TCP implements dynamic sliding window flow control \(rwnd\) and) Tj
T*
(   multi-phase congestion control \(AIMD, Slow Start\). UDP provides no flow or congestion control.) Tj
T*
(   6. Speed & Latency: UDP delivers low latency and minimal overhead, ideal for real-time) Tj
T*
(   applications \(VoIP, DNS, video streaming\). TCP ensures 100% integrity, ideal for web \(HTTP\),) Tj
T*
(   email \(SMTP\), and file transfer \(FTP\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the complete 8-byte UDP header format and describe the purpose and calculation of each field, including the pseudo-header.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The User Datagram Protocol header is remarkably simple, consisting of four 16-bit \(2-byte\)) Tj
T*
(   fields totaling 8 bytes \(64 bits\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Source Port \(16 bits\): Port number of the sending process. If the client does not expect a) Tj
T*
(   reply, this field can optionally be set to 0.) Tj
T*
(   2. Destination Port \(16 bits\): Port number of the intended recipient process, essential for) Tj
T*
(   receiver demultiplexing.) Tj
T*
(   3. Length \(16 bits\): The total length of the UDP datagram in bytes, including the 8-byte header) Tj
T*
(   and the payload data \(Minimum value = 8\).) Tj
T*
(   4. Checksum \(16 bits\): 16-bit one's complement sum of the datagram for bit-error detection.) Tj
T*
(   Optional in IPv4 \(value 0 disables it\), but mandatory in IPv6.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Pseudo-Header Checksum:) Tj
T*
(   To calculate the checksum, a 12-byte temporary pseudo-header is prepended, containing Source) Tj
T*
(   IP, Destination IP, zero padding, Protocol Number \(17 for UDP\), and UDP Length. This verifies) Tj
T*
(   that the datagram was not erroneously delivered to the wrong host IP or transport protocol) Tj
T*
(   handler.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Draw and describe the complete TCP segment header format, explaining the function of each 32-bit word and control flag.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   A standard TCP segment header contains 20 bytes of mandatory fields arranged across five 32-bit) Tj
T*
(   words:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Word 1: Source Port \(16 bits\) and Destination Port \(16 bits\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Word 2: Sequence Number \(32 bits\) - Indicates the byte sequence number of the first payload) Tj
T*
(   byte in this segment, or the Initial Sequence Number \(ISN\) if SYN is set.) Tj
T*
(   3. Word 3: Acknowledgment Number \(32 bits\) - Valid if ACK flag is set; indicates the next) Tj
T*
(   contiguous byte expected from the peer.) Tj
T*
(   4. Word 4: Data Offset / Header Length \(4 bits, specifies header length in 32-bit words\),) Tj
T*
(   Reserved \(3 bits\), Control Flags \(9 bits: NS, CWR, ECE, URG, ACK, PSH, RST, SYN, FIN\), and) Tj
T*
(   Window Size \(16 bits, advertised receive window rwnd\).) Tj
T*
ET

