(   [Problem Statement]: Compare TCP Tahoe, TCP Reno, TCP NewReno, and TCP CUBIC congestion control algorithms.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Different TCP variants employ distinct strategies to handle congestion and maximize throughput:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. TCP Tahoe \(1988\): On any loss \(whether by RTO or 3 duplicate ACKs\), Tahoe sets ssthresh =) Tj
T*
(   cwnd / 2, resets cwnd = 1 MSS, and re-enters Slow Start. This severe penalty creates a) Tj
T*
(   'sawtooth' pattern with prolonged idle periods.) Tj
T*
(   2. TCP Reno \(1990\): Adds Fast Retransmit and Fast Recovery. On 3 duplicate ACKs, it halves cwnd) Tj
T*
(   and stays in Congestion Avoidance, avoiding Slow Start from 1 MSS.) Tj
T*
(   3. TCP NewReno \(1999\): Fixes Reno's deficiency when multiple packets are dropped from a single) Tj
T*
(   window. It remains in Fast Recovery until all segments outstanding at the onset of loss are) Tj
T*
(   acknowledged, avoiding premature re-entry to slow start.) Tj
T*
(   4. TCP CUBIC \(Modern Linux Default\): Replaces linear window growth with a cubic function based) Tj
T*
(   on elapsed time since the last congestion event. CUBIC rapidly ramps up window size in) Tj
T*
(   high-speed, long-delay networks \(LFN\) and is less dependent on short RTTs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 14:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain how TCP calculates and dynamically updates the Retransmission Timeout \(RTO\) using Jacobson's Algorithm.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Because Internet packet delays fluctuate widely, TCP cannot use a fixed RTO. Jacobson's) Tj
T*
(   Algorithm calculates dynamic RTO using Smoothed Round-Trip Time \(SRTT\) and Round-Trip Time) Tj
T*
(   Variation \(RTTVAR\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Measuring SampleRTT: The time elapsed between sending a segment and receiving its ACK.) Tj
T*
(   2. Karn's Algorithm Exception: SampleRTT is never measured for retransmitted segments, avoiding) Tj
T*
(   ambiguity regarding which transmission generated the ACK.) Tj
T*
(   3. Updating SRTT and RTTVAR:) Tj
T*
(   - Difference: Error = SampleRTT - SRTT) Tj
T*
(   - SRTT_new = SRTT_old + \(alpha * Error\), typically alpha = 1/8 \(0.125\)) Tj
T*
(   - RTTVAR_new = RTTVAR_old + \(beta * \(|Error| - RTTVAR_old\)\), typically beta = 1/4 \(0.25\)) Tj
T*
(   4. Computing RTO: RTO = SRTT + \(4 * RTTVAR\). A safety margin of 4 standard deviations) Tj
T*
(   accommodates bursty jitter. Minimum RTO is clamped to 1.0 second \(or 200 ms in modern OS) Tj
T*
(   kernels\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 15:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the Bandwidth-Delay Product \(BDP\) in high-speed networks and explain why TCP Window Scaling \(RFC 1323\) is essential.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Bandwidth-Delay Product \(BDP\) represents the maximum volume of unacknowledged data that can) Tj
T*
(   be 'in flight' across a network path at any given instant:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Formula: BDP = Bottleneck Bandwidth \(bits/sec\) * Round-Trip Time \(seconds\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Example:) Tj
T*
(   For a 10 Gbps transatlantic link with RTT = 100 ms \(0.1 s\):) Tj
T*
(   BDP = 10 * 10^9 * 0.1 = 1,000,000,000 bits = 125 Megabytes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The TCP Bottleneck:) Tj
T*
(   Standard TCP headers allocate only 16 bits for the Window Size field, capping the maximum) Tj
T*
(   advertised receive window at 2^16 - 1 = 65,535 bytes \(~64 KB\). On this 10 Gbps link, a 64 KB) Tj
T*
(   window caps throughput at:) Tj
T*
ET

