12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Calculate the maximum achievable throughput of a TCP connection given: RTT = 40 ms, MSS = 1460 bytes, and packet loss rate p = 0.01%, using Mathis's Formula.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Mathis's Formula models TCP throughput in Congestion Avoidance under steady-state packet loss:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Formula: Throughput <= \(MSS / RTT\) * \(C / sqrt\(p\)\), where C is commonly approximated as 1.22) Tj
T*
(   \(for delayed ACKs\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Given Parameters:) Tj
T*
(   - MSS = 1460 bytes = 1460 * 8 = 11,680 bits) Tj
T*
(   - RTT = 40 ms = 0.040 seconds) Tj
T*
(   - Loss rate p = 0.01% = 0.0001 -> sqrt\(p\) = 0.01) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Calculation:) Tj
T*
(   Throughput <= \(11,680 bits / 0.040 s\) * \(1.22 / 0.01\)) Tj
T*
(   Throughput <= 292,000 bits/sec * 122 = 35,624,000 bits/sec = 35.62 Mbps.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Conclusion: Even with infinite bandwidth, TCP Reno throughput on this link is bounded at ~35.6) Tj
T*
(   Mbps due to round-trip delay and random packet loss.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 22:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the operation and purpose of Explicit Congestion Notification \(ECN, RFC 3168\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   ECN allows intermediate network routers to signal impending buffer congestion to TCP endpoints) Tj
T*
(   without dropping packets:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Mechanism:) Tj
T*
(   1. IP Header Bits: Uses the 2-bit ECN field in the IP header \(bits 6 and 7 of Traffic) Tj
T*
(   Class/TOS\). Endpoints negotiate ECN capability during TCP handshake, setting bits to 10 or 01) Tj
T*
(   \(ECT - ECN-Capable Transport\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Router Marking: When a router's queue exceeds a threshold \(via Active Queue Management /) Tj
T*
(   RED\), it flips the ECN bits to 11 \(CE - Congestion Experienced\) instead of dropping the packet.) Tj
T*
(   3. TCP Echo: The receiving TCP host sees CE=11 and sets the ECE \(ECN-Echo\) flag in its) Tj
T*
(   returning ACK.) Tj
T*
(   4. Congestion Response: The sender treats ECE identical to a packet drop \(halving cwnd\) and) Tj
T*
(   transmits a segment with CWR \(Congestion Window Reduced\) set to acknowledge receipt.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Advantage: Prevents unnecessary packet retransmissions, drastically reducing latency and) Tj
T*
(   bufferbloat.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
