(>> Solution to Question 26:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the problem of Bufferbloat in network routers and how modern queue management \(AQM / CoDel\) mitigates it.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Bufferbloat refers to severe high-latency spikes caused by oversized, unmanaged FIFO buffers in) Tj
T*
(   consumer network routers and broadband modems:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Problem Mechanism:) Tj
T*
(   1. Router manufacturers incorporated massive RAM buffers to prevent packet drops during short) Tj
T*
(   traffic bursts.) Tj
T*
(   2. Standard TCP loss-based congestion control \(Tahoe, Reno, CUBIC\) increases cwnd until a) Tj
T*
(   packet is dropped. With massive buffers, packets are not dropped; instead, they sit in) Tj
T*
(   multi-second queues.) Tj
T*
(   3. Result: Latency explodes from 20 ms to 2000 ms, causing real-time applications \(VoIP, DNS,) Tj
T*
(   online games\) to freeze while bulk file downloads continue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Mitigation via Active Queue Management \(AQM\):) Tj
T*
(   - CoDel \(Controlled Delay\) and FQ-CoDel: Measure the actual time packets dwell in queue rather) Tj
T*
(   than queue length. If dwell time exceeds 5 ms consistently, CoDel preemptively drops \(or) Tj
T*
(   ECN-marks\) packets at the head of the queue, signaling TCP to throttle its cwnd without filling) Tj
T*
(   buffer memory.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 27:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Compare the Transport Layer OSI reference model specifications with the practical TCP/IP implementation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The theoretical OSI model and practical TCP/IP architecture exhibit notable structural) Tj
T*
(   divergences:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Service Classes: OSI specifies five transport service classes \(TP0 to TP4\) designed for) Tj
T*
(   varying network quality \(TP0 for reliable X.25 to TP4 for unreliable networks\). TCP/IP) Tj
T*
(   implements two primary pragmatic protocols: TCP \(equivalent to TP4\) and UDP.) Tj
T*
(   2. Separation of Concerns: OSI cleanly decouples Session \(Layer 5\) and Presentation \(Layer 6\)) Tj
T*
(   from Transport \(Layer 4\). In TCP/IP, session establishment, encryption \(TLS\), compression, and) Tj
T*
(   character encoding are handled directly in user-space Application protocols.) Tj
T*
(   3. Connection-Oriented vs Connectionless: OSI favored connection-oriented virtual circuits at) Tj
T*
(   both Network and Transport layers. TCP/IP implemented a stateless, connectionless Network Layer) Tj
T*
(   \(IP\) paired with an intelligent Transport Layer \(TCP\) responsible for end-to-end state) Tj
T*
(   maintenance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 28:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the sequence of TCP socket API system calls executed on both Client and Server to establish a connection, exchange data, and close.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The standard Berkeley Sockets API workflow proceeds as follows:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Server Side:) Tj
T*
(   1. socket\(\): Creates an endpoint socket file descriptor.) Tj
T*
(   2. bind\(\): Binds the socket to a local IP address and well-known Port number.) Tj
T*
(   3. listen\(\): Marks socket as passive and sets the backlog connection queue size.) Tj
T*
ET

